52 19.5983 22.6117L16.996 14.8432L23.7872 10.0456C23.9206 9.95133 24 9.7981 24 9.63469Z"></path></clipPath><path id="path_RatingStar_undefined0small" d="M24 9.63469C24 9.35683 23.7747 9.13158 23.4969 9.13158H15.0892L12.477 1.34327C12.4269 1.19375 12.3095 1.0764 12.16 1.02625C11.8966 0.937894 11.6114 1.07983 11.523 1.34327L8.91088 9.13158H0.503157C0.33975 9.13158 0.186521 9.21094 0.0922364 9.3444C-0.0680877 9.57134 -0.0140806 9.88529 0.212865 10.0456L7.00408 14.8432L4.40172 22.6166C4.35092 22.7683 4.37534 22.9352 4.46749 23.066C4.6275 23.2932 4.94137 23.3476 5.16853 23.1876L12 18.3758L18.8317 23.183C18.9625 23.2751 19.1293 23.2994 19.281 23.2486C19.5445 23.1604 19.6865 22.8752 19.5983 22.6117L16.996 14.8432L23.7872 10.0456C23.9206 9.95133 24 9.7981 24 9.63469Z"></path></defs><use clip-path="url(#clip_RatingStar_undefined0small)" href="#path_RatingStar_undefined0small" class="RatingStar__backgroundFill"></use><path class="RatingStar__fill"></path></svg></span></span></div><span class="Text Text__body3"><a href="https://www.goodreads.com/review/show/3740378266">April 1, 2022</a></span></section><section class="ReviewText"><section class="ReviewText__content" dir="auto"><div class="TruncatedContent" tabindex="-1"><div class="TruncatedContent__text TruncatedContent__text--large" tabindex="-1" data-testid="contentContainer"><span class="Formatted"><b>Edit:</b> 4 stars is my honest rating, but my more technical rating is 3.75 stars, because this book was too long. I'm being brutally honest with myself and my ratings and going back reevaluating them. I initially rated other books in this series 4 stars, but in all honesty they were more a 3 star read for me because of the length. A valid criticism is these books are too long. <br><br><br>I am left stunned. Alison Aimes' writing is fast-paced, energetic, and vibrant. With her debut Omegaverse book she's already established herself as a top tier Omegaverse author. For those familiar with her Condemned series this won't come as a surprise, but for Omegaverse readers new to Alison Aimes you're going to want to check this one out. The alien/sci fi element is strong--for sci fi romance lovers who may not be attracted to mafia, don't let the mafia element scare you away from the story. I thought the combination was fab. This is basically mafia in outer space. This book is a <b>standalone that ends with a satisfying HEA</b> :) <br><br>Aimes is a new to me author, I requested an ARC when I noticed Ruthless King up for pre-order on Amazon based on the cover and word mafia alone. I was a bit worried the book wouldn't deliver on the promises in the blurb, but there was no need to worry - this book delivers. <br><br>✦ <b>The setting</b><br><br><img src="https://i.gr-assets.com/images/S/compressed.photo.goodreads.com/hostedimages/1610471855i/30685635.jpg" width="500" height="480" alt="description" class="gr-hostedUserImg" loading="lazy"><br><br><b>Anarcheim, a parallel Alphaverse galaxy in a dark future seeded with varied forms of alien life. There are, however, two immutable constants. The first is that all inhabitants are Alpha, beta, or omega. Alphas lead, betas serve, and omegas submit. That is the way. The second is that violence is a way of life, power is essential to survival, and crime is king.</b><br><br>The author did an amazing job of setting up her Omegaverse world, and it's clear enough that I can wholly recommend it to those new to Omegaverse. If you like sci fi romance, but haven't tried Omegaverse yet, this book would be a great place to start. <br><br>The author's descriptions of the various planets and areas within Anarcheim were stunning. I noticed this after reading a couple books in her Condemned series, but she has a way of bringing the setting to life. Much of this book takes place on an icy planet, Abzal, and the descriptions were to die for. <br><br>✦ <b>Nikolai</b><br><br><b>“Your chance for freedom ended fifteen years ago. Your fate set the moment we met.”</b><br><br>Nikolai was swoon worthy. The type of Alpha who is an @hole but ends up caving to his Omega and doing anything to protect her. My favorite mafia aspect of this book was how loyal Nikolai treated his family. He was willing to do anything to avenge the ones he lost and protect the ones he loved. His back story was gut wrenching! Props to the author for writing *that* scene so early in book, my jaw dropped. But going back to family, in Omegaverse there is such emphasis on the Omegas' ability to breed, I'm always surprised to not see as many children or families depicted in the stories. This book depicted how important family lines are within the Brotherhood. <br><br>While I'm shelving this as dark Omegaverse, this is on the lighter side of dark. There are heavy, dark themes, but Nikolai was not a cruel Alpha like in the darkest Omegaverses. He had his moments, he was certainly an asshole at times, but there was an overarching theme of both MCs learning how to respect and trust each other. <br><br>✦ <b>Dahlia</b><br><br><img src="https://i.gr-assets.com/images/S/compressed.photo.goodreads.com/hostedimages/1610468912i/30685418._SY540_.jpg" width="460" height="450" alt="description" class="gr-hostedUserImg" loading="lazy"><br><br>Dahlia was snarky without crossing into bratty territory (something I always appreciate). Those who like strong heroines who push back against the Alpha will like Dahlia. Another pet peeve I have is authors writing 'strong' heroines but all they do is talk back to the hero (it's like the author thinks that's enough to advertise a 'strong heroine'). Dahlia had her own goals, took action to meet them, and came into her own power by the end of the book. <br><br>I wouldn't describe this book as enemies to lovers per se - this ended up being a childhood meet cute to separation to love story which I ate up. Both protagonists come from families with an ongoing blood feud, and Dahlia is key to how Nikolai will enact his revenge upon her family. This created an angsty push and pull between the two. <br><br> <b>“I see.” “I doubt you do. This is going to get uglier before it gets better. War is coming, the full extent of the player involved still to be determined, and you’re at the center of it all."</b><br><br>This is a standalone, but there's still a lot going on this world and I can't wait for more. It looks like each book in this series will be Nikolai's brothers being paired with their Omegas (and I'm eager for what will either be Nikolai's sister or Dahlia's sister's story!). <br><br><br><b>Trigger Warnings</b>:<input type="checkbox" class="spoiler__control" aria-label="The following text has been marked spoiler. Toggle checkbox to reveal or hide." onchange="this.labels[0].setAttribute('aria-hidden', !this.checked);" id="fbd64d58-4940-4fa4-a8c2-6e33ea1f0e9c" /><label aria-hidden="true" class="spoiler" for="fbd64d58-4940-4fa4-a8c2-6e33ea1f0e9c">Violence, murder, references to childhood abuse (brief), and dubcon --&gt;the forced seduction trope, the omega caves to the bond, especially in the beginning, when she doesn't want too</label><br><br><b>Safety</b>: <input type="checkbox" class="spoiler__control" aria-label="The following text has been marked spoiler. Toggle checkbox to reveal or hide." onchange="this.labels[0].setAttribute('aria-hidden', !this.checked);" id="d5c1b2d7-0f01-445f-8f2b-7c6bcc14ce89" /><label aria-hidden="true" class="spoiler" for="d5c1b2d7-0f01-445f-8f2b-7c6bcc14ce89">No cheating, no ow drama. Alpha is possessive and caring of his omega</label><br><br><b>Wordcount</b>: 97K! This is a <u>lengthy</u> book. So many Omegaverse books are on the shorter side, it's awesome to find a quality, full length Omegaverse story. I felt fully satiated after reading. <br><br><b>This is a standalone and ends with a HEA</b> No cliffhanger :)<br><br><br><br>Notes:<input type="checkbox" class="spoiler__control" aria-label="The following text has been marked spoiler. Toggle checkbox to reveal or hide." onchange="this.labels[0].setAttribute('aria-hidden', !this.checked);" id="33b32ffc-181a-446c-bb36-516d2392fce6" /><label aria-hidden="true" class="spoiler" for="33b32ffc-181a-446c-bb36-516d2392fce6">These are just random notes for myself! please don't read if you haven't read the book!<br><br>I liked that it was typical for the Alphas to have an 'Omega Prime' as well as 'Omega Property' kept in stables. I feel Omegaverse reverse harems are highly unrealistic, and think it's more realistic an Alpha would have a harem. No worries for those concerned about safety though! The H doesn't have Omega stables :) <br><br>I really liked how the Omegas gift tied into the fated mate bond, emotionally and physically. How it tied into the Omega Prime status as well.<br><br>I swooned when I learned Nikolai kept the necklace! I love it when there are objects of importance like that in a story! <br><br>I appreciated certain world build details, like how gems had their own name, I cracked up at the phrase, 'Alpha bitch,' similar to how guys don't want to be seen as 'whipped.' <br><br>Plot twist was fab. Well constructed plot with foreshadowing, which is difficult to do. <br><br>Constructive criticism: This is me nitpicking, nothing major at all needs to be changed. <br><br>During dialogue, at one point Nikolai said: “What does the fated-mate bond tell you?” @63% in the story. This is serious nitpicking, but saying the full thing, 'fated mate bond,' seemed slightly unnatural and like a mouthful. I would think it would be more natural for him to shorten it to bond. <br><br>There were times in the story where there could have been more subtlety and nuances of a scene left to the reader to interpret, but I felt instead the nuances and implications of a scene were spelled out to the reader. This is criticism to take with a grain of salt, because I think the majority of readers like having that--but here's an example of what I'm referring too: <br><br>@53%: <br><br><i>Equally bad was the memory of how, for a few heartbeats before he fucked it up, her gaze had been warm and welcoming, the golden glow flowing from her to him along the deepening fated-mate bond making him feel lighter than he had in ages—until the vision and his knee-jerk angry reaction messed everything up. He’d struck out, wanting to prove he was strong, despite what she’d seen. All he’d done was prove what an asshole he really was.</i><br><br>This was a really great scene, before the reader had experienced it from Dahlia's point of view, and how she felt hated/resented by Nikolai for experiencing a vision that he wanted no one else to see. <br><br>Then the book switches to Nikolai's POV and we learn he is feeling regret for his reaction to Dahlia. <br><br>I was able to recognize Nikolai's regret pretty quickly. This nuance was explained to the reader a few times, that by the time I got to the paragraph above, it felt slightly repetitious. I think not all of it was necessary and a few sentences could have been edited out. This is very minor, me nitpicking, but something I would mention to an author if I was beta reading. Personal taste comes into play too--I like being able to read between the lines a bit and pay attention to subtext. I think there could be room for the author to balance this a little bit more. <br><br>One more thing I liked about the world:<br><br>How the balance between the Brotherhood alliance was shaky and easily unbalanced. Alphas are *so* aggressive &amp; territorial, that it seemed very realistic that the Brotherhood would need to take all kinds of precautions to keep the Alphas from murdering each other. All the details from the glass encasements at the meetings, to serious consequences for the Brotherhood killing other members etc was great. I also appreciated how there was variety among the Omegas personality, some scheming, some sociopaths, some nurturing/kind, they all had personalities and their own interests. <br><br></label></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></["br"]></span></div><div class=""></div></div></section></section><section class="ReviewCard__tags"><ul class="CollapsableList"><span tabindex="-1"><a href="https://www.goodreads.com/review/list/81374210?shelf=aliens" class="Button Button--tag Button--medium"><span class="Button__labelItem">aliens</span></a><span tabindex="-1"> </span><a href="https://www.goodreads.com/review/list/81374210?shelf=faves-omegaverse" class="Button Button--tag Button--medium"><span class="Button__labelItem">faves-omegaverse</span></a><span tabindex="-1"> </span><a href="https://www.goodreads.com/review/list/81374210?shelf=mafia" class="Button Button--tag Button--medium"><span class="Button__labelItem">mafia</span></a></span><span tabindex="-1"></span> <div class="Button__container"><button type="button" class="Button Button--tag Button--medium" aria-label="Show all items in the list"><span class="Button__labelItem">...more</span></button></div></ul></section><footer class="SocialFooter"><div data-testid="stats" class="SocialFooter__statsContainer"><div class="Button__container"><button type="button" class="Button Button--inline Button--medium Button--subdued"><span class="Button__labelItem">60 likes</span></button></div><div class="Button__container"><button type="button" class="Button Button--inline Button--medium Button--subdued">