83C18.9625 23.2751 19.1293 23.2994 19.281 23.2486C19.5445 23.1604 19.6865 22.8752 19.5983 22.6117L16.996 14.8432L23.7872 10.0456C23.9206 9.95133 24 9.7981 24 9.63469Z"></path></defs><use clip-path="url(#clip_RatingStar_undefined0small)" href="#path_RatingStar_undefined0small" class="RatingStar__backgroundFill"></use><path class="RatingStar__fill"></path></svg></span></span></div><span class="Text Text__body3"><a href="https://www.goodreads.com/review/show/3776596092">April 15, 2021</a></span></section><section class="ReviewText"><section class="ReviewText__content" dir="auto"><div class="TruncatedContent" tabindex="-1"><div class="TruncatedContent__text TruncatedContent__text--large" tabindex="-1" data-testid="contentContainer"><span class="Formatted">This is the first in the author's Omegaverse/Sci-Fi/Mafia series centering on five siblings, their efforts to avenge childhood tragedies, and the partners they meet along the way. While it's a standalone in terms of the MC, certain lines of action will continue in subsequent books. While this has "dark" moments (the H's memories of past traumas), he doesn't harm his Omega. Side Note: In terms of genre, I don't understand the difference between "Fated Mates" and "Omegaverse;" I realize that the former can apply to other sub-genres, such as "Shifter."<br /><br />Here, we get family head Alpha Nikolai and his efforts to indict the rival mob boss he thinks killed members of his family. Part of his revenge plan includes taking the latter's Omega daughter, Dahlia, rumored to have the gift of second sight that will enable Nikolai to finally prove her father's guilt. If he starts to have feelings for her, well, that's not going to deter him -- or will it?<br /><br />I enjoyed and highly recommend this book, and will be reading the rest of the series. Five aspects in particular appealed to me:<br /><br />1) The worldbuilding is excellent: the geographical, economic, political, and social aspects are defined, detailed, and well-explained. <br /><br />2) While all the usual Omegaverse tropes exist (heat, knotting), Ms. Aimes' intervention -- each Omega possesses a "gift" similar to an X-Men superpower -- allows Dahlia more agency and power than is usual in this genre. In addition, the "cinematic" nature of Dahlia's sight was an interesting angle. Side Note: Her race's physical characteristics resemble Tokein's elves.<br /><br />3) The conflict between the couple is seemingly insurmountable since both are loyal to their own families; this creates organic, high-stakes tension. <br /><br />4) Both were likeable, fully-fleshed out characters with complex backstories, and achieved growth. <br /><br />5) The prose was well-edited with no errors that I saw and detailed enough without turning into info-dump.<br /><br />I did have two problems. First, the length is unnecessary: after a while, Nikolai and Dahlia's back-and-forth became repetitious as we heard more than once about his inability to feel and hers to trust. Second, the ending was too stuffed with OTT plot points: <input type="checkbox" class="spoiler__control" aria-label="The following text has been marked spoiler. Toggle checkbox to reveal or hide." onchange="this.labels[0].setAttribute('aria-hidden', !this.checked);" id="945cb5de-cde1-46f3-9566-9a81fadc6baa" /><label aria-hidden="true" class="spoiler" for="945cb5de-cde1-46f3-9566-9a81fadc6baa">battles, big reveals, murders</label>. Often authors think that more is "more," when actually it's often "too much," and, ironically, "not enough" since the sheer multitude of events and speed at which they unfold don't allow for development. In this case, for example, given all the story space devoted to Dahlia's father, it felt anti-climactic <input type="checkbox" class="spoiler__control" aria-label="The following text has been marked spoiler. Toggle checkbox to reveal or hide." onchange="this.labels[0].setAttribute('aria-hidden', !this.checked);" id="8846e6fe-4a1f-4b99-a0e0-d92251722ba0" /><label aria-hidden="true" class="spoiler" for="8846e6fe-4a1f-4b99-a0e0-d92251722ba0">when his off-page death was reported. And perhaps because I just read this plot twist in another book, I'm over the "Faking The Dead" trope -- the Skolov family had enough motivation to continue searching for the killers without that unnecessary incentive </label>.<br /><br />4 stars. While the twist at the end isn't a complete surprise given the hints we're given, it was still clever. I believe we'll get one of the brothers' stories next.</span></div><div class=""></div></div></section></section><section class="ReviewCard__tags"><ul class="CollapsableList"><span tabindex="-1"><a href="https://www.goodreads.com/review/list/98512744?shelf=descriptive" class="Button Button--tag Button--medium"><span class="Button__labelItem">descriptive</span></a><span tabindex="-1"> </span><a href="https://www.goodreads.com/review/list/98512744?shelf=imaginative" class="Button Button--tag Button--medium"><span class="Button__labelItem">imaginative</span></a><span tabindex="-1"> </span><a href="https://www.goodreads.com/review/list/98512744?shelf=interesting" class="Button Button--tag Button--medium"><span class="Button__labelItem">interesting</span></a></span><span tabindex="-1"></span> <div class="Button__container"><button type="button" class="Button Button--tag Button--medium" aria-label="Show all items in the list"><span class="Button__labelItem">...more</span></button></div></ul></section><footer class="SocialFooter"><div data-testid="stats" class="SocialFooter__statsContainer"><div class="Button__container"><button type="button" class="Button Button--inline Button--medium Button--subdued"><span class="Button__labelItem">8 likes</span></button></div></div><div data-testid="actions"><div class="SocialFooter__action"><div class="Button__container"><button type="button" class="Button Button--inline Button--medium" aria-label="Review unliked. Press to like review"><span class="Button__labelItem"><i cl